607
00:39:50,296 --> 00:39:51,348
Scott: And so I try to go down there.

608
00:39:52,811 --> 00:39:57,929
Scott: And there may have been something I missed, another way to get into the bottom right area of the map.

609
00:39:58,131 --> 00:40:05,848
Scott: But on the overworld, the bottom right area of the map was pretty much sealed tight with, like, a wall of trees and mountains all along the edge.

610
00:40:06,770 --> 00:40:15,510
Scott: And it's like, yeah, there's various ways of using the items you can summon, crates and beds and trampolines, but you can only summon so many at once.

611
00:40:16,032 --> 00:40:19,270
Scott: It's not like there's unlimited crates where you can just make a mountain of them.

612
00:40:19,550 --> 00:40:27,409
Scott: It only lets you, depending on how much you've leveled yourself up and how many other dungeons you've done, your limits on how many you can make might be low.

613
00:40:28,232 --> 00:40:29,729
Scott: So it's like, well, how can I get in there?

614
00:40:30,171 --> 00:40:35,670
Scott: And I thought I was pretty clever, and I did, like, creatively found a way to get just enough height.

615
00:40:36,570 --> 00:40:41,668
Scott: And it felt like I wasn't supposed to be where I was, like, up on top of some trees.

616
00:40:42,973 --> 00:40:45,268
Scott: Like, this can't be the official entrance, can it?

617
00:40:45,751 --> 00:40:47,130
Scott: Feels like the wrong entrance.

618
00:40:47,491 --> 00:40:52,070
Scott: Like, I pulled off a fast one, but the game didn't care.

619
00:40:52,110 --> 00:40:52,608
Scott: It just worked.

620
00:40:53,451 --> 00:40:57,090
Rym: And I like that, because that's, you know, Tears of the Kingdom and Breath of the Wild also had that feeling.

621
00:40:57,151 --> 00:40:58,187
Rym: We don't care how you get there.

622
00:40:58,772 --> 00:40:59,389
Rym: It's right there.

623
00:40:59,512 --> 00:41:00,150
Rym: That's the puzzle.

624
00:41:00,231 --> 00:41:00,705
Rym: Can you get in?

625
00:41:00,990 --> 00:41:01,080
Rym: Cool.

626
00:41:02,070 --> 00:41:02,157
Scott: Yeah.

627
00:41:02,470 --> 00:41:09,570
Scott: I don't, you know, maybe I did go in, like, the intended way, because, you know, the other areas that I had tried to get in were much more difficult.

628
00:41:09,650 --> 00:41:13,090
Scott: And I found, like, this one area where I could just barely get enough height.

629
00:41:13,190 --> 00:41:15,190
Scott: Maybe that was the right entrance, right?

630
00:41:15,230 --> 00:41:17,130
Scott: And I did, or maybe it wasn't.

631
00:41:17,411 --> 00:41:23,049
Scott: Maybe they expected you to go a different way, or they accounted for every different way you might go in.

632
00:41:24,052 --> 00:41:25,790
Scott: But everything worked out just fine.

633
00:41:26,032 --> 00:41:28,970
Scott: The game, you know, the plot proceeded accordingly.

634
00:41:30,010 --> 00:41:30,602
Scott: And I was like, "Oh,

635
00:41:30,827 --> 00:41:30,990
Scott: okay.".

636
00:41:32,112 --> 00:41:43,427
Scott: And that's the thing, is that the fun of the game is that, a lot of times, it's either showing you something that looks so impossible, like, "How can I possibly do that, even with all these crazy

637
00:41:43,548 --> 00:41:43,750
Scott: items?".

638
00:41:44,115 --> 00:41:44,989
Scott: But there is a way.

639
00:41:45,671 --> 00:41:47,050
Scott: You know there has to be a way, right?

640
00:41:47,150 --> 00:41:48,049
Scott: Why would they show it to you?

641
00:41:48,110 --> 00:41:48,242
Rym: Yep.

642
00:41:48,410 --> 00:41:49,976
Rym: And the game is good at bounding.

643
00:41:50,157 --> 00:41:53,170
Rym: what ways would even be remotely feasible to try.

644
00:41:53,592 --> 00:41:56,169
Rym: So you're not, like, trying something that's completely fruitless.

645
00:41:57,183 --> 00:41:57,369
Scott: Yeah, yeah.

646
00:41:57,711 --> 00:42:06,890
Scott: And at the same time, there are times where you can figure out a way to do something that feels like you're being naughty, and you're getting away with one, right?

647
00:42:06,972 --> 00:42:07,869
Scott: And so that's fun, too.

648
00:42:07,970 --> 00:42:09,249
Scott: And the same thing with the bad guys.

649
00:42:09,750 --> 00:42:14,450
Scott: Fighting bad guys, you can just turn into Link and fight the bad guys normally.

650
00:42:14,590 --> 00:42:17,970
Rym: Yep, you get plenty of that energy to do that if you really want to do that.

651
00:42:18,251 --> 00:42:21,110
Scott: Yeah, you can do it, and sometimes it's the best way to do it.

652
00:42:21,472 --> 00:42:26,650
Scott: Really, what you want to do is summon enemies to fight for you, and then just sit and wait like a necromancer in Diablo.

653
00:42:26,870 --> 00:42:27,488
Scott: That's way more fun.

654
00:42:28,050 --> 00:42:29,670
Rym: Even in the beginning, you get so many enemies.

655
00:42:29,910 --> 00:42:35,970
Rym: Like, right away, the first thing I did is summon three bats and have them follow me around, and that's still pretty effective for a while.

656
00:42:36,572 --> 00:42:43,489
Scott: And depending on the opponent enemies, different strategies of which enemies you're going to summon to fight with them will be more effective, you know?

657
00:42:43,851 --> 00:42:47,090
Scott: If the enemies are made of fire, you probably don't want to summon Gibdo.

658
00:42:48,470 --> 00:42:48,702
Scott: Right?

659
00:42:48,990 --> 00:42:50,730
Scott: That's probably not going to work, you know?

660
00:42:51,231 --> 00:42:56,030
Scott: If the enemies are knights with armor, you probably don't want to summon Moblins throwing spears.

661
00:42:56,310 --> 00:42:57,710
Scott: They're kind of going to bounce off, right?

662
00:42:57,730 --> 00:42:58,470
Scott: It's not going to work out to you.

663
00:42:58,470 --> 00:43:04,650
Rym: I really like how they let you encounter a pretty strong enemy really early and get the summon.

664
00:43:05,110 --> 00:43:07,825
Rym: Like, you get the Darknut, and then you're like, "I can just summon

665
00:43:07,845 --> 00:43:07,947
Rym: him?".

666
00:43:08,150 --> 00:43:12,090
Rym: And you summon him, and he's fucking badass in the beginning of the game with a giant axe.

667
00:43:12,650 --> 00:43:13,829
Scott: Yeah, and then soon enough, he's garbage.

668
00:43:14,351 --> 00:43:15,330
Rym: Yep, he's garbage pretty quick.

669
00:43:15,831 --> 00:43:21,603
Rym: Well, you said as soon as you get Zelda 3 spinning a chain over my head guy, Yeah.

670
00:43:22,832 --> 00:43:24,330
Rym: Actually, I've been having a lot of fun with the P-hat.

671
00:43:24,490 --> 00:43:26,028
Rym: The P-hat just chases goblins down.

672
00:43:26,591 --> 00:43:26,930
Rym: Or Moblins down.

673
00:43:27,430 --> 00:43:28,289
Scott: Where do you get Wizrobe?

674
00:43:28,973 --> 00:43:30,230
Rym: Oh, Wizrobe is always my favorite.

675
00:43:31,272 --> 00:43:32,829
Scott: Ah, there's different kinds of Wizrobes, too.

676
00:43:33,512 --> 00:43:35,950
Rym: No, in the original Zelda, there was blue, and there was orange.

677
00:43:36,610 --> 00:43:42,469
Scott: I also, when you're in the water, very few things beat shark, but there is some case to use piranha.

678
00:43:43,970 --> 00:43:50,370
Scott: But yeah, it's like you have all these bad guys you can summon to fight for you, or some of the bad guys do things that aren't fighting.

679
00:43:50,471 --> 00:43:52,390
Scott: The spider is the earliest one in the game.

680
00:43:52,891 --> 00:44:04,830
Scott: When you summon the spider in the 2D sort of side view areas, the spider makes a web going up, and you can jump and climb on the web, and it's very useful for getting around and getting higher elevation.

681
00:44:04,991 --> 00:44:07,290
Rym: In the 3D areas, it also works.

682
00:44:07,450 --> 00:44:10,090
Rym: It hooks to an invisible ceiling in a lot of places.

683
00:44:10,130 --> 00:44:12,230
Scott: Yeah, but it's not as useful as the 2D area.

684
00:44:12,250 --> 00:44:17,129
Rym: I used it a few times already to climb up and get onto something that I otherwise couldn't easily get onto.

685
00:44:17,931 --> 00:44:34,289
Scott: Hmm, it's not completely useless, but it's not as... But yeah, it's like, hey, I don't know if it has the insane creative flexibility that the Tears of the Kingdom has, because it doesn't have the physics engine going on in there.

686
00:44:36,231 --> 00:44:42,930
Scott: You're basically just summoning game objects into the world that is already built to accommodate game objects.

687
00:44:43,553 --> 00:44:49,770
Scott: So there is a lot of creativity, but it doesn't go overboard where nonsense can go on.

688
00:44:49,950 --> 00:44:56,430
Rym: Yeah, but at the same time, that keeps it a little more constrained so that the puzzles can be fairly 2D Zelda puzzle-y in a way that is satisfying.

689
00:44:57,150 --> 00:45:01,550
Scott: It would be boring if everything became so easy because you could just cheese your way through the game.

690
00:45:01,710 --> 00:45:03,310
Scott: Like, oh, I beat Ganon with a bed.

691
00:45:03,472 --> 00:45:04,269
Scott: It's like, that's funny.

692
00:45:04,871 --> 00:45:11,509
Scott: But it would be kind of anti-climactic in a way if you did it too easily.

693
00:45:11,852 --> 00:45:17,570
Scott: And it does a good balance of that, especially with the enemies, is that sometimes if you're clever, you could just blow enemies away.

694
00:45:17,810 --> 00:45:19,710
Scott: Like, ha ha ha ha ha, you're so weak.

695
00:45:20,152 --> 00:45:25,329
Scott: And then other times it's like, yeah, really, no matter what you do, you're kind of going to have to fight this enemy, especially the bosses.

696
00:45:26,913 --> 00:45:31,290
Scott: I haven't found a way to cheese a boss and just beat it instantly or something.

697
00:45:31,610 --> 00:45:36,749
Scott: You always have to kind of do something to deal with it, follow its pattern of some kind.

698
00:45:37,431 --> 00:45:46,127
Rym: There is one interesting, because I noted this quality of life thing that this game does, that I remember being a frustration in the original Link's Awakening when I replayed it.

699
00:45:47,252 --> 00:45:49,830
Rym: There is fast travel fucking everywhere in this game.

700
00:45:49,970 --> 00:45:51,624
Rym: You find one, like, every few feet.

701
00:45:51,664 --> 00:45:52,410
Rym: there's a fast travel.

702
00:45:52,690 --> 00:45:56,250
Rym: You can fast travel to anywhere you've ever been, like, so easily.

703
00:45:56,654 --> 00:45:57,988
Rym: So you can just be where you want to be.

704
00:45:58,891 --> 00:46:09,365
Scott: And, I mean, it's, you know, don't waste time making someone, like, you know, earlier Zelda games, it's like, okay, you can fast travel once you get the flute or once you get the ocarina, right?

705
00:46:10,051 --> 00:46:11,690
Scott: Until then, you got to walk everywhere.

706
00:46:11,890 --> 00:46:14,870
Scott: This game's like, ah, fuck it, we don't even need an item.

707
00:46:15,070 --> 00:46:18,869
Scott: You just, if you hit a checkpoint, you can teleport between checkpoints for free at the end.

708
00:46:19,132 --> 00:46:19,290
Rym: Yep.

709
00:46:20,210 --> 00:46:28,530
Rym: In fact, in the first dungeon, the one they make you go into to sort of teach you what's going on with the rifts, they, there's multiple fast travel points even inside the dungeon.

710
00:46:28,912 --> 00:46:34,149
Rym: There's one right next to the boss door, and then you find the boss key and you're like, okay, I guess I'll just teleport straight to the boss door.

711
00:46:35,135 --> 00:46:35,270
Scott: Yep.

712
00:46:37,412 --> 00:46:46,790
Scott: So, yeah, I don't know how long the game is, because on the one hand, like, it could be that I've only got two more dungeons and then the boss final dungeon.

713
00:46:47,812 --> 00:46:51,650
Scott: But if that's the case, that means I'm only gonna get three more heart containers.

714
00:46:52,053 --> 00:46:59,048
Scott: and I look at my heart container meter and it's like, and I look at how much healing the various potions do in terms of how many hearts they heal.

715
00:46:59,210 --> 00:47:01,631
Rym: Oh, yeah, the first potion I made heals like 13.

716
00:47:01,631 --> 00:47:09,230
Scott: It's like, yeah, and it's like, either there's a lot more pieces of heart that I'm missing because I am getting quite a few pieces of heart.

717
00:47:10,113 --> 00:47:13,530
Scott: It's possible that I just, there's way more that I'm still not getting.

718
00:47:13,650 --> 00:47:17,130
Rym: Or maybe it's just number three of beat these dungeons to then beat these dungeons.

719
00:47:17,712 --> 00:47:23,330
Scott: It could be that there are even more dungeons than I suspect in this game, which would be pretty great, actually.

720
00:47:23,350 --> 00:47:28,989
Scott: If there was one more set of dungeons, because I already, I cleared out like the first set and I'm on the second set.

721
00:47:29,671 --> 00:47:34,810
Scott: If there was a third set, that would be like the ideal number of dungeons, it would be so great.

722
00:47:35,233 --> 00:47:45,989
Scott: If the second set is the final set, it's like, that would be sort of expected, but a little bit, you know, leaving me wanting a little bit more.

723
00:47:47,431 --> 00:47:48,686
Rym: The internet seems to agree.

724
00:47:48,727 --> 00:47:50,830
Rym: the game is about a 20-ish hour game.

725
00:47:51,530 --> 00:47:57,730
Scott: I don't know how many hours I've spent, but basically every time I sit down to play, I beat a dungeon and then I put it down.

726
00:47:58,190 --> 00:48:02,950
Rym: The game, I would say, I would characterize the game as not being hard, like it's pretty easy.

727
00:48:02,970 --> 00:48:08,550
Scott: No, it's way easy, especially because you don't need to do the action combat if you don't want to.

728
00:48:08,690 --> 00:48:10,330
Scott: You can just let enemies do it all for you.

729
00:48:10,550 --> 00:48:14,809
Rym: You really just need to figure out the puzzle, which mostly is, how do I get there?

730
00:48:15,151 --> 00:48:16,070
Rym: How do I hit that switch?

731
00:48:16,491 --> 00:48:17,870
Rym: How do I get through this area?

732
00:48:18,270 --> 00:48:19,770
Rym: Like, it's that kind of game, mostly.

733
00:48:19,930 --> 00:48:26,970
Scott: Nothing is challenging to the video game hands, except maybe the bosses and even then, not really.

734
00:48:28,470 --> 00:48:33,010
Scott: But some of the puzzles are a little bit brain-teasy, even for adults, so.

735
00:48:33,090 --> 00:48:33,950
Rym: But I appreciate that.

736
00:48:34,212 --> 00:48:43,389
Rym: Some games, you have to figure out a solution and execute with timing, but then there's games where you just have to figure out the solution, like those Lolo games.

737
00:48:44,331 --> 00:48:48,809
Rym: As an old example from the NES days, if you figured out the solution, the execution was pretty easy.

738
00:48:49,150 --> 00:48:50,150
Scott: Figuring out the solution was the hard part.

739
00:48:50,170 --> 00:48:52,190
Scott: - Yeah, Lolo's a Sokoban, so it's all the same.

740
00:48:53,614 --> 00:48:54,769
Rym: But I'm enjoying this game.

741
00:48:56,076 --> 00:48:56,670
Rym: It's beautiful.

742
00:48:56,870 --> 00:48:58,350
Rym: It looks just like, I mean, it's the same engine.

743
00:48:58,410 --> 00:49:16,090
Rym: It looks just like The Link's Awakening, though I did notice it actually gets, it drops its frame rate a lot along the way, and I feel like this game will also be released on Ounce, because I feel like it is pushing the limits of what pre-Ounce is capable of doing.

744
00:49:16,650 --> 00:49:27,589
Scott: - Well, I think it's pretty clear by now that we know that if they've got this eShop, they've got the Nintendo Switch online, everyone has a count, Nintendo accounts.

745
00:49:28,175 --> 00:49:33,930
Scott: When you log into the Ounce, you're gonna be able, all your digital games are gonna be there, right?

746
00:49:34,231 --> 00:49:40,870
Scott: It's gonna be probably, I would be surprised if it wasn't fully backward compatible with the Switch, so you won't need your Switch anymore.

747
00:49:41,711 --> 00:49:46,109
Scott: And there are just gonna be a lot of games early next year, whenever this console comes out.

748
00:49:46,731 --> 00:49:49,249
Scott: I presume the Pokemon one, which they seem to have leaked.

749
00:49:49,690 --> 00:49:58,930
Scott: Also, I presume, based on just educated knowledge, but not actual knowledge, that Metroid Prime 4 also will be on both consoles.

750
00:49:59,210 --> 00:50:05,929
Scott: Nintendo's done this in the past, where they've had games as recently as Breath of the Wild, which were dual console games.

751
00:50:06,750 --> 00:50:12,989
Scott: And so there's gonna be a bunch of dual console releases, and there's gonna be eventually exclusives.

752
00:50:14,071 --> 00:50:28,630
Scott: But I think a lot of third-party developers making games, just go in the eShop, it's like, as long as the game doesn't require the advanced hardware power of the newer console, why would you make it not work on Switch?

753
00:50:28,890 --> 00:50:30,530
Scott: Why would you make it exclusive to the new one?

754
00:50:30,610 --> 00:50:33,890
Scott: You're just costing yourself money, considering how many Switch consoles are out in the world.

755
00:50:35,292 --> 00:50:36,488
Scott: So that's how it's gonna be.

756
00:50:37,230 --> 00:50:42,289
Rym: - So yeah, this is, if you like Zelda games, I feel like this is just an obvious buy.

757
00:50:43,893 --> 00:50:46,470
Rym: I can't think of anyone who likes Zelda games who won't like this game.

758
00:50:47,530 --> 00:50:52,950
Scott: - Yeah, I mean, if you like a 2D Zelda, it's not some one of the bad, like there's the bad train one or whatever.

759
00:50:54,293 --> 00:50:55,789
Scott: And this is better than a lot of them.

760
00:50:56,751 --> 00:50:58,660
Scott: It's not a waste of your $60 or $50 if you use the voucher.

761
00:51:01,390 --> 00:51:08,048
Rym: - Also interestingly, it canonically, in the context of this game itself, Link is mute.

762
00:51:08,370 --> 00:51:09,430
Rym: Like Link cannot speak.

763
00:51:09,631 --> 00:51:10,729
Rym: That is like talked about.

764
00:51:11,192 --> 00:51:12,610
Rym: It is part of the story.

765
00:51:13,030 --> 00:51:13,909
Rym: Characters reference it.

766
00:51:14,290 --> 00:51:17,509
Rym: Link is just mute as a person, and the game just, that's a matter of fact.

767
00:51:18,310 --> 00:51:21,949
Scott: - It is funny how plot-wise in this one, Zelda and Link don't even know each other.

768
00:51:22,571 --> 00:51:37,450
Scott: But it's like, Zelda finds out about Link, and there's all these other people in Hyrule who know Link, and Zelda is like learning about Link from these other villagers and Hyruleans who are like, "Oh yeah, that adventurer kid, "he promised my kid he'd bring him a toy, "but he hasn't come back.

769
00:51:37,550 --> 00:51:38,361
Scott: "I wonder what happened to

770
00:51:38,421 --> 00:51:38,482
Scott: him.".

771
00:51:39,130 --> 00:51:46,950
Scott: And like, Zelda sees Link like once or twice, but like doesn't know him, and they've never met effectively speaking.

772
00:51:47,170 --> 00:51:48,449
Rym: - I guess we should take a moment too.

773
00:51:48,590 --> 00:51:53,810
Rym: I wanna highlight, when you play this game, early, early on, you're gonna see a wanted poster for Zelda.

774
00:51:54,271 --> 00:51:56,069
Rym: That is an A+ wanted poster.

775
00:51:56,910 --> 00:52:06,587
Scott: - Well, not only is that A+, the detail, because it's like a 2D game, and they, you know, it's not 2D, but it's not, you know, it's a 3/4 overhead view.

776
00:52:07,250 --> 00:52:11,010
Scott: They didn't have to put in like insane, high resolution details into everything.

777
00:52:11,671 --> 00:52:19,769
Scott: There are a lot of really subtle touches, especially in places like the villagers' homes, or people's rooms.

778
00:52:20,310 --> 00:52:25,410
Scott: Like, there's the lady, of course, if you go to Kakariko Village, there's the woman with the escaped cuckoos.

779
00:52:25,790 --> 00:52:26,988
Rym: - I was about to bring her up.

780
00:52:27,410 --> 00:52:28,629
Scott: - You gotta throw them in the fence.

781
00:52:28,792 --> 00:52:29,410
Scott: It's a classic.

782
00:52:29,611 --> 00:52:30,968
Scott: Of course you have to do that, right?

783
00:52:31,613 --> 00:52:32,410
Scott: It's a Zelda game.

784
00:52:32,650 --> 00:52:33,629
Scott: You gotta collect the cuckoos.

785
00:52:34,090 --> 00:52:40,690
Scott: You go in her house, and on her bedside nightstand is photos, there's like a photo of her holding a cuckoo, right?

786
00:52:40,890 --> 00:52:53,589
Scott: It's like, all these small details, in everyone's house, and on the villagers' clothing, and all these around, they put in small things like that all over the place.

787
00:52:55,632 --> 00:53:01,190
Scott: That kind of Disney World level of attention to detail really adds a lot of life to the game.

788
00:53:01,730 --> 00:53:02,110
Rym: - Absolutely does.

789
00:53:02,230 --> 00:53:05,470
Rym: And I think, take your time to just sort of poke around and look at stuff.

790
00:53:05,710 --> 00:53:07,670
Rym: Don't just laser focus on solving the puzzles.

791
00:53:08,413 --> 00:53:11,730
Rym: Go into all the places, like any good person who plays a Zelda game should.

792
00:53:11,810 --> 00:53:12,850
Rym: You should see every pixel.

793
00:53:14,710 --> 00:53:15,169
Scott: - Yeah.

794
00:53:15,533 --> 00:53:23,790
Scott: Oh, the other thing is, another example is like, you go to the desert area, and it's like, there's a desert in the bottom left of the map.

795
00:53:23,891 --> 00:53:25,129
Scott: Where have I seen that before?

796
00:53:25,570 --> 00:53:26,709
